<p>Netflix has dropped out of its pursuit of Warner Bros. Discovery, saying it will not raise its offer to match Paramount Skydance’s improved proposal. That clears the way for Paramount’s David Ellison to take control of the media group behind Warner Bros., CNN and HBO. Warner Bros. Discovery said Paramount’s revised proposal offers $31 per share in cash, and Netflix said it would not meet those terms. The decision ends a months-long bidding battle that would have been Netflix’s first big push to buy a legacy studio-and-TV conglomerate, and it puts a huge mix of film, TV and news assets on track to sit under one corporate roof. Deal terms also bring some clarity: Netflix is set to receive a $2.8 billion breakup fee, and Paramount’s bid includes a larger termination fee if regulators block the transaction—signalling both sides are focused on closing rather than dragging out the fight.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/JAQSGxKEMrt2l6oQm8gC7iwkrDw9-hJ7DxnxotREOoMt1-QLlnzoeLBBvgKdajKL8uSno_XyAtVH0wajAy8KDe_OmuPVbcqqteNv439cydLrDXMvGjz0kg-EfbeMOLPGzvrGIlEhr0QjmSn1coouoLJjDQj51a_BuTG9W5yspUM-Ix6T-l0CYj689YJ2nEd550AKXFplbSaUZkDJt91tdOepfjrVLIieQDlRTfguzUHUVtQsux6QqEpENA' alt='The Warner Bros water tower at Warner Bros Studios in Burbank, California (Dec. 5, 2025).' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Deal values: Reports pegged Paramount Skydance’s improved cash offer at about $108 billion or $111 billion—numbers that helped frame Netflix’s choice not to bid higher.</li><li>Regulatory hedges: Paramount raised the termination fee it would pay if regulators reject the deal to $7 billion from $5.8 billion, according to Warner Bros. Discovery’s notice to Netflix.</li><li>Political backdrop: Semafor said Netflix faced “dismal odds” in Washington, and linked the deal environment to President Trump’s stance— including his criticism of Netflix board member Susan Rice after her comments about companies that “take the knee” to Trump.</li><li>Ellison influence: The SCMP said the outcome would put properties ranging from CNN to HBO under the Ellison family’s influence, led by Oracle founder Larry Ellison and his son David Ellison.</li></ul><blockquote>This transaction was always a ‘nice to have’ at the right price, not a ‘must have’ at any price, - Ted Sarandos and Greg Peters</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Netflix: Co-CEOs Ted Sarandos and Greg Peters said Netflix was “declining to match” Paramount’s latest offer, arguing the price required to do so made the deal unattractive. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/media/2026/feb/26/netflix-paramount-warner-bros'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Warner Bros. Discovery: CEO David Zaslav said the company was excited about the potential combination with Paramount Skydance and “can’t wait to get started working together telling the stories that move the world.” (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/02/26/2026/netflix-says-no-thank-you-to-warner-bros-discovery'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>Paramount Skydance: Paramount cast its revised bid as the stronger offer, triggering a contractual window for Netflix to respond before it stepped aside. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/paramount-raises-the-stakes-in-battle-with-netflix/'>michaelwest.com.au</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3344795/netflix-abandons-warner-bros-bid-clearing-way-paramount'>Paramount poised to acquire Warner Bros after Netflix drops bid</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/paramount-raises-the-stakes-in-battle-with-netflix/'>Paramount raises the stakes in battle with Netflix</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/02/26/2026/netflix-says-no-thank-you-to-warner-bros-discovery'>Netflix walks away from $83B Warner Bros. takeover</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5y6p5ypgmzo'>Netflix drops bid for Warner Bros, clearing way for Paramount takeover</a> - bbc.com</li>